"Executive summary\n- There is a complete L3 reachability failure between remote-worker (10.1.0.2) and server (10.0.1.2) in both directions (100% ICMP loss).\n- The server\u2019s local LAN and default next hop (branch-fw inside, 10.0.1.1) are up and reachable; routing on the server is correct.\n- Traceroute from the server toward 10.1.0.2 returns no hops at all, pointing to a drop/suppress at the first routed device (branch firewall) rather than an issue on the server LAN.\n- The highest-probability fault domain is the branch firewall\u2019s forwarding/policy/routing between 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/30.\n\nKey evidence and correlations\n- Bidirectional failure: Pings fail both from remote-worker\u2192server and server\u2192remote-worker (100% loss). This rules out a simple one-way routing issue on the server alone.\n- Server-side health and configuration:\n  - eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP, correctly addressed 10.0.1.2/24; no local NIC errors noted.\n  - ip route shows a specific route to 10.1.0.0/30 via 10.0.1.1 on eth0; default route via eth1 is less specific and not in play for 10.1.0.0/30.\n  - Ping to 10.0.1.1 (branch-fw inside) succeeds with 0% loss and low RTT, confirming the 10.0.1.0/24 segment and the firewall\u2019s inside interface are operational.\n- Forwarding path symptoms:\n  - Traceroute (ICMP) to 10.1.0.2 returns no hop responses (no TTL-exceeded from hop 1). Since the first hop should be 10.0.1.1, this strongly suggests the firewall is not forwarding and/or not generating/sending ICMP Time Exceeded for transit traffic.\n  - An ARP entry to 10.0.1.1 was observed in DELAY (probes 1). While this can be transient and later resolved (given ping to 10.0.1.1 succeeded), it hints at recent lack of confirmed reachability to the gateway prior to the ping test.\n- Missing visibility:\n  - Attempts to list host firewall rules failed (sudo: nft/iptables not found or exit 1). This leaves host-based filtering unknown, but Linux hosts rarely block all outbound ICMP by default, and the successful ping to 10.0.1.1 argues against a host egress block.\n  - Multiple command timeouts/errors (ip neigh, ip route, ip addr, traceroute on other nodes) limited end-to-end validation on intermediate devices.\n\nMost likely issues (with details)\n1) Branch firewall policy blocking or not forwarding transit traffic between 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/30\n   - Indicators: 100% loss both ways; server can reach firewall\u2019s inside IP; traceroute sees no hop 1; implies the firewall is dropping or not forwarding ICMP and possibly other traffic between zones.\n   - Nuance: Many firewalls may respond to direct ICMP to the interface (ping 10.0.1.1 succeeds) but still drop transit ICMP (and not send TTL-exceeded). This exactly matches the observed pattern.\n\n2) Branch firewall or branch router routing misconfiguration/asymmetry\n   - Indicators: Only the server\u2019s route table was verified; we have no confirmed route on branch-fw for 10.1.0.0/30 or on branch-router for 10.0.1.0/24. Missing or wrong routes could cause drops at the firewall.\n   - Nuance: Even with a routing issue further downstream, traceroute should usually elicit a TTL-exceeded from the first hop; the absence of hop 1 replies still points at the firewall\u2019s behavior, but routing on the firewall should be verified alongside policy.\n\n3) ICMP-specific filtering vs. general forwarding failure\n   - Indicators: We tested primarily ICMP (ping/traceroute). It is possible non-ICMP is allowed/denied differently. However, zero traceroute hop replies suggest control-plane ICMP for TTL-expired may also be suppressed or dropped by policy.\n   - Next step: Validate TCP/UDP reachability (e.g., simple TCP test) to determine if the failure is ICMP-only or general.\n\n4) Transient/unstable ARP adjacency to the firewall\n   - Indicators: Neighbor 10.0.1.1 seen in NUD DELAY with probes. This state can occur during reachability probing or after inactivity. Since direct pings to 10.0.1.1 later succeed, any ARP instability may be intermittent or timing-related.\n   - Impact: Unlikely to be the root cause of end-to-end failure, but worth confirming no duplicate IP/MAC or L2 issues exist on the 10.0.1.0/24 segment.\n\n5) Incomplete host visibility (tooling/permissions)\n   - Indicators: nft/iptables not present; BusyBox ip option mismatch; timeouts on several commands.\n   - Impact: Hinders definitive confirmation that the host firewall is not interfering. Given the evidence, host firewall is unlikely to be the cause, but verifying it would remove doubt.\n\nRecommended remediation and next steps (in priority order)\n- On the branch firewall (10.0.1.1 inside):\n  - Verify inter-zone policy between 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/30. Ensure allow rules for ICMP echo/echo-reply and, if needed, application traffic. Temporarily permit any-any between these two subnets to test.\n  - Confirm routing: \n    - Route to 10.1.0.0/30 via the correct next hop/interface toward the branch router.\n    - Return route from the branch router/remote-worker back to 10.0.1.0/24 is present and symmetric.\n  - Check NAT/masquerade rules that might interfere with inter-subnet traffic; disable or adjust if unnecessary for these internal networks.\n  - Inspect counters/logs:\n    - Enable logging on drop rules for these subnets; look for ICMP drops.\n    - If available, use a \u201cpacket-tracer\u201d/simulation feature to trace ICMP from 10.0.1.2 to 10.1.0.2 and vice versa to identify the dropping policy.\n  - Validate control-plane behavior:\n    - Confirm the firewall is configured to generate/send ICMP Time Exceeded for transit traffic and not to silently drop all ICMP.\n\n- On the branch router (toward remote-worker):\n  - Verify routes: Presence of 10.0.1.0/24 via the branch firewall, and correct adjacency on the 10.1.0.0/30 link.\n  - Check ACLs/filters on the router interfaces for denies affecting 10.0.1.0/24 <-> 10.1.0.0/30.\n\n- On the server (10.0.1.2):\n  - Capture traffic to confirm path break location:\n    - Run tcpdump on eth0 while pinging 10.1.0.2. You should see ICMP echo requests egress and no replies. Also watch for any ICMP Time Exceeded from 10.0.1.1; absence supports the firewall-drop hypothesis.\n  - Re-check neighbor state:\n    - ip neigh show 10.0.1.1 and observe transitions to REACHABLE after pinging. Investigate if it remains in DELAY/STALE abnormally (could indicate L2 issues or duplicate IP/MAC).\n  - Confirm no host-level block:\n    - Verify nftables/iptables configuration (install tools if needed) and sysctl icmp settings (net.ipv4.icmp_echo_ignore_all should be 0). Host egress blocks are unlikely but easy to rule out.\n\n- Functional tests to isolate ICMP vs general forwarding:\n  - From server to remote-worker, attempt a TCP connection test (e.g., tcping or netcat to a known open port if any). If TCP also fails, the issue is general forwarding/policy; if TCP succeeds while ICMP fails, then ICMP-specific filtering is the culprit.\n\n- Improve troubleshooting visibility:\n  - Ensure proper tooling and permissions on involved hosts: install iproute2-full, traceroute, nftables/iptables; correct sudo PATH and increase command timeouts.\n  - Collect configs and logs on the firewall for policies/routes/NAT and any drop counters specifically for:\n    - src 10.0.1.2 \u2192 dst 10.1.0.2 (ICMP type 8)\n    - src 10.1.0.2 \u2192 dst 10.0.1.2 (ICMP type 0)\n    - ICMP type 11 (Time Exceeded)\n\nWhat to fix most likely\n- Adjust branch firewall to permit and forward traffic between 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/30 (start with ICMP, then the required application protocols). Ensure correct routes on the firewall and branch router for both subnets. Once forwarding is enabled and symmetric routing is confirmed, pings and traceroute should immediately begin to show hop 1 (10.0.1.1) and reach 10.1.0.2."